<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chao Phraya River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chao Phraya is the main river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, formed at Nakhon Sawan by the meeting of the Ping and the Nan and flowing south past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. Its fertile flood plain supported the Dvaravati towns, Lavo and Suphannaphum, and the successive Thai capitals of Ayutthaya, Thonburi and Bangkok, whose wealth rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and on trade carried along the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river.jpg" alt="Chao Phraya River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chao_Phraya_River_-_Bangkok_-_northwards_-_from_King_Taksin_Bridge_-_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
